# WireMesh controller configuration (systemd EnvironmentFile).
# The controller is entirely env-configured. Defaults shown; uncomment to change.
# The controller's OWN state dir (CA key/cert, the SQLite DB, secrets/) — the
# FRESH-INSTALL default, deliberately split from the gateway's
# /var/lib/wiremesh so the two can share a host: that directory belongs to the
# gateway (root-owned 0700, and the gateway unit drops CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE), so a
# controller running as `wiremesh` there either can't read it or steals it from
# the gateway. The unit's StateDirectory=wiremesh-controller creates this path
# owned wiremesh:wiremesh.
#
# UPGRADING from a release that defaulted to /var/lib/wiremesh? Your state
# STAYS THERE and the package pins this line back to /var/lib/wiremesh for you.
# Nothing is ever moved automatically — a maintainer script cannot safely
# relocate a live controller's SQLite DB, and getting it half-done used to mean
# a regenerated CA and a re-enrollment of the whole fabric. Splitting a
# co-located host is a deliberate, service-down procedure: see docs/install.md.
#
# Why the pin is needed at all: BOTH package formats replace this file on
# upgrade when you have not edited it. deb has no conffile "noreplace"
# mechanism at all, and rpm's %config(noreplace) only protects a file whose
# checksum differs from the one the old package shipped. So on an unmodified
# config, deb and rpm alike silently install this new version — pointing at
# the new directory — and the postinstall then puts your path back.
#
# CONSEQUENCE OF THE PIN, worth knowing: once the postinstall has edited this
# file, the package manager considers it locally modified.
#   - deb: the next release that changes this file will PROMPT you
#     ("Modified (by you or by a script) since installation").
#   - rpm: the next release that changes this file will NOT touch yours; it
#     writes /etc/wiremesh/controller.env.rpmnew instead, SILENTLY. New
#     settings we ship will not reach you until you diff that file in. If you
#     run rpm hosts, check for *.rpmnew after every wiremesh-controller
#     upgrade.
WIREMESH_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/wiremesh-controller
# Bind enroll/sync/observe to all interfaces so remote gateways can reach them.
# (Admin TCP is ALWAYS loopback-only regardless of this.)
WIREMESH_BIND_IP=0.0.0.0
# WIREMESH_TCP_PORT=9400          # enrollment (server-TLS)
# WIREMESH_SYNC_TCP_PORT=9500     # Sync (mTLS)
# WIREMESH_OBSERVE_UDP_PORT=9600  # endpoint observation
# WIREMESH_ADMIN_TCP_PORT=9443    # admin (loopback-only)
